    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Max arrives at Victoria's apartment and awkwardly asks to move in with her. He tries to sound independent, but eventually admits his true feelings. Let us listen to their conversation in Russian first.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Привет, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Привет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хочу переехать к тебе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тебе негде жить?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, у меня миллион вариантов.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Костя зовёт меня назад.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лёва тоже приглашает жить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но я готов жить с тобой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Что ты хочешь сказать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хочу жить с тобой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не хочу быть без тебя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Это очень приятно слышать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Правда?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Да, я тебе верю.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Привет, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hi, Max.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'hi' straight to 'Max'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Привет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hi.' A crisp, everyday conversational reply meaning 'hi' that you will hear constantly in Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хочу переехать к тебе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I want to move to your place.' Max uses the informal singular 'you' here with 'for you', as friends and close colleagues do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тебе негде жить?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you have nowhere to live?' Vika combines 'for you' with 'to live' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, у меня миллион вариантов.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, I have a million options.' Notice how 'no' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'options'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Костя зовёт меня назад.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Kostya is calling me back.' — Notice the proper name meaning 'Kostya' paired with the verb for 'calls'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Лёва тоже приглашает жить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Leva is also inviting me to live.' — Notice the proper name meaning 'Lyova' paired with the adverb for 'too'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но я готов жить с тобой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But I am ready to live with you.' Max uses the informal singular 'you' here with 'you', as friends and close colleagues do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Что ты хочешь сказать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What do you want to say?' The question opens with the interrogative word for 'what' and builds toward 'tell'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хочу жить с тобой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I want to live with you.' Max links 'I' directly with 'want' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я не хочу быть без тебя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I do not want to be without you.' Notice how 'I' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Это очень приятно слышать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That is very pleasant to hear.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'this' with 'to hear'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Правда?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Really?' Max asks this with a single word meaning 'honestly', using rising intonation to turn it into a quick question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Да, я тебе верю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, I believe you.' Notice how 'yes' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'believe'. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
